1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:01,440
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:08,220
Today we'll be looking at how this amplifier attachment for the LabSignal Generator works for testing our coils.

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,380
There's nothing complicated about it.

4
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:24,580
It's built around an IRF840 transistor with a TC4420 driver for that transistor on the heatsink and a go-between chip linking the LabSignal Generator and the driver.

5
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:30,440
The go-between chip is the same one as before, a TC4093BP.

6
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:38,160
To power these chips, we use a DC-DC converter built around the LM2596.

7
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:44,360
We set it to 15V with the trim pot and feed at a slightly higher voltage.

8
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:47,900
In my case, it's an 18V mains converter.

9
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:52,420
It can run from batteries too, whatever you have on hand.

10
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:55,760
The schematic is shown in the picture.

11
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:05,480
By the way, I'll upload all my schematics to a file sharing service and drop the link under this video, same as the one I filmed before.

12
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,600
At this point, you should see a nice clean oscillogram like this.

13
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:14,060
Let's see what else.

14
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:24,100
The signal from the LabSignal Generator hits one of the Schmitt triggers, it's inverted, and then we invert it again on the second Schmitt trigger to get the true signal.

15
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:31,360
From there, through a 10 ohms damping resistor, the signal reaches the driver controlling the FET's gate.

16
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:39,960
Then the FET switches our main power from batteries or any other power source onto the inductor that drives our coil.

17
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:47,890
The wiring isn't complicated.

18
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:49,350
Let's look at how it works.

19
00:01:52,010 --> 00:01:55,550
First, this system connects to the LabSignal Generator.

20
00:01:56,830 --> 00:01:59,730
The LabSignal Generator gets switched to pulse mode.

21
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:02,410
Then you set the pulse frequency on the control.

22
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:08,430
Our circuit works up to 2.5MHz.

23
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:11,070
After that, we set the signal amplitude.

24
00:02:13,330 --> 00:02:15,470
Signal amplitude is 6V.

25
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:18,370
That's the minimum this attachment will work at.

26
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:24,810
If you have a 5V signal generator, you'll need to swap this Schmitt trigger chip for 5V logic.

27
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:27,610
It's also compatible with this driver.

28
00:02:29,910 --> 00:02:36,410
You just need to add an L7805 regulator after this converter for the 5V logic supply.

29
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:40,230
And the circuit will work just fine.

30
00:02:42,690 --> 00:02:46,530
Once the amplitude is set, the next thing is the duty cycle.

31
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:49,850
Right now duty cycle is at 73%.

32
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:52,170
Let me show you what the duty cycle does.

33
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:55,950
I feed power from the supply to the transistor.

34
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:05,600
We see the oscillogram.

35
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:07,840
This is what you should get.

36
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:13,120
Now watch, I'm going to change the duty cycle.

37
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:15,680
In this case, decreasing it.

38
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,560
See, the signal becomes unclean.

39
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:28,520
So for each coil we have to dial in the signal individually, along with the transistor's operating point at that frequency.

40
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,640
That's the duty cycle adjustment.

41
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:37,400
Here I dropped it to 10%, bringing it back to a clean signal.

42
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:44,100
In this case at this frequency, it starts working around 71%.

43
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:48,940
Once we've got a clean signal, we start probing our coil.

44
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:53,180
What do we get?

45
00:03:53,880 --> 00:04:03,180
With this kind of drive, with the LC resonance frequency for this coil set at 1.68MHz, the coils start behaving just like a Tesla coil.

46
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:10,760
Right now both ends are in the air, it's not grounded, and you can see there's a discharge.

47
00:04:16,510 --> 00:04:19,150
Basically the same kind of corona as a Tesla.

48
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:31,210
The coil's working in quarter wave mode.

49
00:04:31,650 --> 00:04:33,630
You can tell by how the neon bulb glows.

50
00:04:35,570 --> 00:04:38,250
Closer to the hot, and the glow gets brighter.

51
00:04:39,790 --> 00:04:42,210
There's a fluorescent tube parallel to the coil.

52
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:45,270
To light it up, we touch the hot into its lead.

53
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:52,500
I touched the hot into it, and you can see the lamp lights up brightly.

54
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:56,560
After disconnecting, the lamp glows dimly.

55
00:04:58,180 --> 00:04:59,880
And what should we see on this lamp?

56
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:05,840
The camera might not pick it up, but there are stripes running along the lamp.

57
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:07,500
The whole tube goes straight.

58
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:13,120
And if the coil is wound correctly, those stripes will be sharply defined.

59
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:18,580
You want to find the frequency on the lab signal generator where the stripes stop moving entirely.

60
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:23,340
If that happens, the coil is properly built.

61
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:30,600
If you can't get the stripes to stop by changing the frequency, and they keep moving, then the coil is wound slightly wrong.

62
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:34,380
You're not getting a clean longitudinal standing wave around the coil.

63
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:41,800
Around this coil, you'd need to tweak it.

64
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,540
Either increase or decrease the wire length.

65
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:46,740
That's a matter of trial and error.

66
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,960
But the main goal is for the coil to start up and behave like a Tesla transformer.

67
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:57,100
The neons and gas discharge lamps should light up.

68
00:05:57,340 --> 00:06:00,360
And most importantly, you want to see those stripes on the lamp.

69
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,260
The signal again.

